{
  "dom_id": "step-q79428",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-6,9)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-6,9)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3\\cdot (-6),9)=(-18,9),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
